WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

2
00:00:25.068 --> 00:00:27.679
Previously on
Star Trek: Strange New Worlds...

3
00:00:27.723 --> 00:00:29.986
My sacrifice will save the livs

4
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.430
of those I care most about.

5
00:00:31.455 --> 00:00:34.094
- It's time for me to go.
- I've let something out.

6
00:00:34.119 --> 00:00:36.142
- It's your emotions.
- I can't control it.

7
00:00:36.166 --> 00:00:38.516
Mr. Kirk. clean your mess.

8
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.084
T'Pring and I have decided
to take time apart.

9
00:00:41.128 --> 00:00:42.001
I am conflicted

10
00:00:42.026 --> 00:00:43.628
because I have feelings
for someone else.

11
00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:46.133
I don't want to suppress
this one any longer.

12
00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.763
Those events were never
supposed to happen

13
00:00:48.787 --> 00:00:50.746
and you were never meant
to be aware of them.

14
00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:52.617
I have to insist
that you not share

15
00:00:52.661 --> 00:00:55.098
any of what you experienced
from this time.

16
00:00:55.142 --> 00:00:57.274
Buy me a drink next time
we're on starbase together.

17
00:00:57.318 --> 00:00:58.928
That. uh. sounds lovely.

18
00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:03.280
The people you love the most
can cause you the most pain.

19
00:01:03.324 --> 00:01:05.345
but it's the people you love
that can mend your heart

20
00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:07.806
when you feel broken.

21
00:01:11.201 --> 00:01:15.901
Communications Officer's Log.
stardate 2394.8.

22
00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:18.382
Enterprise has been sent
to Bannon's Nebula.

23
00:01:18.426 --> 00:01:20.689
on the edge of explored space.

24
00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:24.258
I've seen a lot of nebulas.
but this one's special.

25
00:01:24.301 --> 00:01:26.173
It's a stellar nursery.

26
00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:28.914
the birthplace of new stars.

27
00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:31.003
But that's not why we're here.

28
00:01:31.047 --> 00:01:34.703
The nursery is also full
of deuterium... starship fuel.

29
00:01:34.746 --> 00:01:38.098
Starfleet's building an outpost
here to collect and refine it.

30
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.839
if they can ever get it workin.

31
00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:45.061
Amazing to think that
a million years from now.

32
00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:46.976
those brand-new stars
will shine down

33
00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:48.804
on explorers just like us.

34
00:01:48.847 --> 00:01:51.023
but this place will look
entirely different by then.

35
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:54.940
Uhura.
You awake there. Ensign?

36
00:01:54.984 --> 00:01:57.595
My eyes are
technically open. Captain.

37
00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:01.643
Just didn't get much sleep
last night.

38
00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:03.123
Or the last ten.

39
00:02:04.646 --> 00:02:06.537
Oh. Well. I appreciate
all the work you've put into

40
00:02:06.561 --> 00:02:08.345
coordinating this mission.

41
00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:10.366
but you generally only want
to burn the candle at one end.

42
00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.001
I'll take that under advisement.

43
00:02:13.698 --> 00:02:15.744
The crew of the refinery
sends their greetings

44
00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:17.833
and they're waiting
for our engineering details.

45
00:02:17.876 --> 00:02:19.878
Have Chief Kyle stand by
to initiate transport.

46
00:02:22.316 --> 00:02:25.449
I hear congratulations
are in order. Fleet Captain.

47
00:02:25.493 --> 00:02:27.451
- Stop it.
- Fleet Captain?

48
00:02:27.495 --> 00:02:30.062
I've been given command of
the refinery and the Farragut.

49
00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:31.760
just until we get
the station on line.

50
00:02:31.803 --> 00:02:35.067
Well. it's the shiniest
gas station I've ever seen.

51
00:02:35.111 --> 00:02:37.418
Well. it's not just
a gas station. Lieutenant.

52
00:02:37.461 --> 00:02:39.028
It's right at the edge
of the frontier.

53
00:02:39.071 --> 00:02:40.334
It unlocks half the quadrant.

54
00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:41.770
It'll be the jumping-off point

55
00:02:41.813 --> 00:02:43.269
for the next great age
of exploration.

56
00:02:43.293 --> 00:02:45.555
Oh. good. I was afraid
I'd miss the speech.

57
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.038
Well. I started earlier
but Uhura fell asleep.

58
00:02:50.257 --> 00:02:52.215
I assumed Starfleet's
primary motivation

59
00:02:52.259 --> 00:02:54.216
was the nebula's proximity
to Gorn space.

60
00:02:54.261 --> 00:02:56.263
Sadly. you're probably right.

61
00:02:56.305 --> 00:02:58.458
Their presence is growing.
This will help us counter it.

62
00:02:58.482 --> 00:03:00.199
Which is why the refinery
needs to be finished

63
00:03:00.223 --> 00:03:02.245
- as soon as possible.
- While the refinery
is off-line.

64
00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:04.271
Enterprise'scollectors
are operational.

65
00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:05.924
though the process
may be slower.

66
00:03:05.968 --> 00:03:08.425
It would be logical to use this
opportunity to refuel ourselves.

67
00:03:08.449 --> 00:03:10.538
My thoughts exactly. Mr. Spock.

68
00:03:10.581 --> 00:03:13.845
Ortegas. find us a dense pocket
of deuterium and do some laps.

69
00:03:13.889 --> 00:03:16.065
Rip some doughnuts through
the gas cloud. aye. sir.

70
00:03:16.108 --> 00:03:18.174
Nice and slow. we don't want to
use more fuel than we collect.

71
00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.591
Activating Bussard collectors.

72
00:03:30.297 --> 00:03:32.299
Refueling underway. Captain.

73
00:03:34.779 --> 00:03:37.956
Sorry to interrupt. Captain.
I'm receiving a signal.

74
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:38.870
On speakers.

75
00:03:43.048 --> 00:03:44.267
Any time. Ensign.

76
00:03:44.311 --> 00:03:45.660
Uh...

77
00:03:46.748 --> 00:03:49.403
- It's gone.
- Can you play back
the recording?

78
00:03:49.446 --> 00:03:51.796
I can't find it. sir.

79
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.103
It was there. Captain.

80
00:03:54.146 --> 00:03:56.081
- It's probably just a glitch...
- I'll run a full diagnostic

81
00:03:56.105 --> 00:03:58.020
on the communication system.

82
00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:03.417
First. disable
the subspace antenna.

83
00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.593
Check for a charge
on the EPS manifold

84
00:04:05.636 --> 00:04:08.726
before disconnecting this tap.

85
00:04:10.293 --> 00:04:11.338
Perfect.

86
00:04:12.817 --> 00:04:14.341
Now. if there's

87
00:04:14.384 --> 00:04:16.386
any residual ionization.

88
00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.519
- here is where you'll find it.
- Uh.

89
00:04:18.562 --> 00:04:20.172
what the hell

90
00:04:20.216 --> 00:04:22.653
- are you doing
inside my nacelle?
- Sorry.

91
00:04:22.697 --> 00:04:26.396
I am recalibrating
the communications array.

92
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.007
The antenna assembly
goes through the nacelles.

93
00:04:29.051 --> 00:04:31.575
I like to be thorough.
and I've asked for so many

94
00:04:31.619 --> 00:04:34.970
of these recalibrations that
eventually Hemmer got annoyed

95
00:04:35.013 --> 00:04:38.278
and said it would be easier
to show me how to do it myself.

96
00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:41.716
You know.
"teach a cadet to fish."

97
00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:43.935
So we made a recording.

98
00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:47.330
Hemmer was our chief engineer.
before he...

99
00:04:47.374 --> 00:04:51.813
I know.
He was one of my best students.

100
00:04:54.381 --> 00:04:57.034
I'm sorry. I just said that
because he's dead.

101
00:04:57.079 --> 00:04:58.907
Actually. he was just okay.

102
00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.998
But look...
Look what he made of himself.

103
00:05:04.086 --> 00:05:05.783
Right.

104
00:05:05.827 --> 00:05:07.481
Anyway. I'm almost done.

105
00:05:10.222 --> 00:05:13.051
How come you never
talked to me before?

106
00:05:14.183 --> 00:05:17.229
Uh. I'm... I'm sure I have.

107
00:05:17.273 --> 00:05:18.448
Oh. no.

108
00:05:18.492 --> 00:05:20.624
- No. no.
- Oh.

109
00:05:22.191 --> 00:05:24.106
I'm sorry.

110
00:05:24.149 --> 00:05:26.369
I've just been busy. I guess.

111
00:05:26.413 --> 00:05:27.849
Welcome to the Enterprise.

112
00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.552
here is where you'll find i.

113
00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:39.382
Now. what do you do next?

114
00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.040
I don't know.

115
00:05:46.955 --> 00:05:50.654
I won't let you
blow up the ship. I promise.

116
00:05:53.178 --> 00:05:55.224
Not that!

117
00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.489
- Important lesson:
be less gullible.
- Hey!

118
00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:01.709
Back to work.

119
00:06:01.752 --> 00:06:05.103
Let's start by turning down the
gain on the subspace antenna...

120
00:06:11.806 --> 00:06:13.851
Between us and the bulkhead.

121
00:06:13.895 --> 00:06:15.853
the whole project's
been a fiasco.

122
00:06:15.897 --> 00:06:18.160
The refinery was supposed
to be on line two months ago.

123
00:06:18.203 --> 00:06:20.292
What's slowing it down?

124
00:06:20.336 --> 00:06:22.773
"Organizational difficulties"

125
00:06:22.817 --> 00:06:25.341
was the polite term
that Starfleet used.

126
00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:28.475
Their previous superintendent
got shipped back to Earth.

127
00:06:28.518 --> 00:06:32.304
which is why they could
really use your version of...

128
00:06:32.348 --> 00:06:34.002
managerial fervor?

129
00:06:34.045 --> 00:06:37.353
Mm! Nothing beats cleaning up
somebody else's mess.

130
00:07:06.034 --> 00:07:07.383
Space.

131
00:07:08.645 --> 00:07:10.908
The final frontier.

132
00:07:12.736 --> 00:07:16.392
These are the voyages
of the starshipEnterprise.

133
00:07:18.525 --> 00:07:20.352
Its five-year mission:

134
00:07:20.396 --> 00:07:23.486
to explore strange new worlds..

135
00:07:25.532 --> 00:07:28.056
To seek out new life

136
00:07:28.099 --> 00:07:30.711
and new civilizations.

137
00:07:31.755 --> 00:07:35.759
to boldly go
where no one has gone before.

138
00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:55.360
Based on what you heard and saw.

139
00:08:55.404 --> 00:08:58.015
and the elevated readings
from your visual cortex.

140
00:08:58.059 --> 00:09:01.105
I'd say it's pretty clear
you were hallucinating.

141
00:09:02.933 --> 00:09:05.545
You said you had just watched
a video of Hemmer.

142
00:09:05.588 --> 00:09:08.069
His death was fresh
in your mind.

143
00:09:09.113 --> 00:09:11.159
Starfleet officers
aren't supposed to

144
00:09:11.202 --> 00:09:12.595
randomly hallucinate.

145
00:09:12.639 --> 00:09:14.379
You didn't do anything wrong.

146
00:09:14.423 --> 00:09:16.773
And there was nothing
random about it.

147
00:09:16.817 --> 00:09:18.732
While working in the nacelle
you were exposed

148
00:09:18.775 --> 00:09:21.648
to a small amount
of refined deuterium.

149
00:09:21.691 --> 00:09:24.041
which can be dangerous.

150
00:09:24.085 --> 00:09:26.566
You have a mild case
of deuterium poisoning.

151
00:09:27.958 --> 00:09:30.134
And that can cause
terrifying hallucinations?

152
00:09:30.178 --> 00:09:31.614
It can.

153
00:09:31.658 --> 00:09:33.137
You could also
experience headaches.

154
00:09:33.181 --> 00:09:36.401
blurred vision. nausea.

155
00:09:38.665 --> 00:09:40.362
This should help
with the symptoms.

156
00:09:43.104 --> 00:09:45.019
But there is
something else here.

157
00:09:45.062 --> 00:09:47.108
Your serotonin
and cortisol levels.

158
00:09:48.631 --> 00:09:49.763
You haven't been sleeping.

159
00:09:49.806 --> 00:09:51.765
Oh.

160
00:09:51.808 --> 00:09:54.376
It's been a busy...

161
00:09:54.419 --> 00:09:55.856
year.

162
00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:57.684
Exhaustion can exacerbate

163
00:09:57.727 --> 00:09:59.511
the effects
of deuterium poisoning.

164
00:09:59.555 --> 00:10:01.296
You need to rest.

165
00:10:01.339 --> 00:10:03.428
How am I supposed to sleep
after what I just saw?

166
00:10:03.472 --> 00:10:05.517
I empathize.

167
00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:10.784
But I also know a stubborn
patient when I see one.

168
00:10:12.394 --> 00:10:13.743
You aren't going back on duty

169
00:10:13.787 --> 00:10:15.440
until I'm satisfied
you've slept.

170
00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.023
Head to Deck Seven.

171
00:10:33.067 --> 00:10:34.895
get to work stabilizing
the life-support grid.

172
00:10:34.938 --> 00:10:36.505
Aye. sir.

173
00:10:40.074 --> 00:10:42.337
It's not as bad as it looks.

174
00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:43.773
It's way worse.

175
00:10:43.817 --> 00:10:45.098
The central refining
and storage systems

176
00:10:45.122 --> 00:10:46.863
all seem to be in good shape.

177
00:10:46.907 --> 00:10:49.474
Have you looked
at the maintenance logs?

178
00:10:49.518 --> 00:10:52.303
Because you get the feeling
that this place has been

179
00:10:52.347 --> 00:10:54.523
breaking down a lot faster

180
00:10:54.566 --> 00:10:57.221
than the crew is able to fix it.

181
00:10:57.265 --> 00:10:59.267
Luckily. we have
three crews now.

182
00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.704
Enterpriseand Farragut'steams
can mop up the problem systems

183
00:11:01.748 --> 00:11:03.967
while the station's teams
get the refinery running.

184
00:11:04.011 --> 00:11:06.317
Yes.
But if there is

185
00:11:06.361 --> 00:11:08.102
an underlying issue.

186
00:11:08.145 --> 00:11:10.539
and it smells like there is.

187
00:11:10.582 --> 00:11:12.280
we ought to figure that out.

188
00:11:12.323 --> 00:11:14.717
I typically don't base
my decision-making

189
00:11:14.761 --> 00:11:16.850
off of feelings and smells.

190
00:11:16.893 --> 00:11:19.679
Oh.

191
00:11:23.813 --> 00:11:25.815
Any underlying issues
will become clear as we work.

192
00:11:25.859 --> 00:11:29.514
Mm.
Aye aye. Commander.

193
00:11:59.066 --> 00:12:01.155
Hello?

194
00:12:33.927 --> 00:12:36.016
- Sam.
- Jimmy.

195
00:12:36.059 --> 00:12:37.713
Welcome to the Enterprise.

196
00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.804
Hell of a ship.

197
00:12:42.892 --> 00:12:44.285
And they let you work here?

198
00:12:44.328 --> 00:12:46.243
What can I say?
Captain Pike

199
00:12:46.287 --> 00:12:48.158
asked for the more handsome
Kirk brother.

200
00:12:51.248 --> 00:12:53.381
You want a tour?

201
00:12:53.424 --> 00:12:55.209
As long as it ends at the bar.

202
00:13:01.215 --> 00:13:03.870
So. what's new and exciting

203
00:13:03.913 --> 00:13:06.176
in the world
of xenoanthropology?

204
00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:07.874
Literally everything
in xenoanthropology

205
00:13:07.917 --> 00:13:09.136
is new and exciting.

206
00:13:10.485 --> 00:13:12.400
But instead of giving you

207
00:13:12.443 --> 00:13:15.229
a long answer
you don't actually care about.

208
00:13:15.272 --> 00:13:16.578
why don't we skip to your news?

209
00:13:17.579 --> 00:13:18.841
Wow.

210
00:13:18.885 --> 00:13:21.148
Sensing some
hostility there. Sam.

211
00:13:21.191 --> 00:13:23.193
Not at all.

212
00:13:23.237 --> 00:13:26.240
Congrats. Jim.
First officer of the Farragut.

213
00:13:26.283 --> 00:13:28.895
Well. not for
another few months.

214
00:13:28.938 --> 00:13:30.766
I still have to train
my replacement.

215
00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.812
Still. it's quite
an achievement.

216
00:13:33.856 --> 00:13:35.379
Thank you.

217
00:13:35.423 --> 00:13:37.686
And youngest first officer
in the whole fleet.

218
00:13:37.729 --> 00:13:40.428
Youngest first officer ever.
actually.

219
00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.887
I don't suppose you remember
who held the record before you?

220
00:13:44.911 --> 00:13:47.000
Ah. okay.
That's what this is.

221
00:13:47.043 --> 00:13:50.220
It was George Kirk. Sr.
First officer of the Kelvin.

222
00:13:50.264 --> 00:13:51.569
Dad gave you his first name.

223
00:13:51.613 --> 00:13:53.006
even if you choose
not to use it.

224
00:13:53.049 --> 00:13:55.747
so I have to do something
to keep up.

225
00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.228
You're making me look...

226
00:13:59.229 --> 00:14:00.796
Like what?

227
00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.929
Sam. what does my ambition
have to do with you?

228
00:14:05.148 --> 00:14:07.890
Dad has a very
old-fashioned idea

229
00:14:07.934 --> 00:14:09.979
of what a successful career
looks like.

230
00:14:10.023 --> 00:14:12.112
what a fulfilling life
looks like.

231
00:14:12.155 --> 00:14:15.028
and it just so happens
it looks a lot like you.

232
00:14:15.071 --> 00:14:17.857
Well. if you're so worried
about impressing Dad.

233
00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:20.163
then why are you wallowing
in a science lab?

234
00:14:20.207 --> 00:14:22.339
Wallowing. seriously?

235
00:14:22.383 --> 00:14:24.341
Records are made to be broken.

236
00:14:24.385 --> 00:14:27.823
I put in some legwork
to beat this one. So what?

237
00:14:27.867 --> 00:14:30.043
We can make the old man proud
in our own ways.

238
00:14:30.086 --> 00:14:32.132
Or not at all.

239
00:14:46.929 --> 00:14:48.757
Finally! Okay.

240
00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.368
life support's been stabilized.

241
00:14:51.412 --> 00:14:54.632
Are you kidding me?

242
00:14:54.676 --> 00:14:56.634
We need to send a team
to Deck Five

243
00:14:56.678 --> 00:14:58.375
to get the EPS regulators
back on line.

244
00:14:58.419 --> 00:15:01.074
Yes. Well. that is going
to have to wait.

245
00:15:01.117 --> 00:15:05.208
One of my teams found something
in the fuel distribution system.

246
00:15:05.252 --> 00:15:08.037
That system was on line already.
What were you...

247
00:15:08.995 --> 00:15:10.735
You disobeyed my direct orders

248
00:15:10.779 --> 00:15:12.626
and went digging around
in the functioning systems...

249
00:15:12.650 --> 00:15:15.653
If I had followed your orders
to the letter.

250
00:15:15.697 --> 00:15:18.961
we would never have found
evidence of sabotage.

251
00:15:19.005 --> 00:15:20.528
Sabotage?

252
00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.534
Your move.

253
00:15:27.709 --> 00:15:29.493
I know.

254
00:15:29.537 --> 00:15:32.540
Is that not a polite euphemism
for "make your move faster"?

255
00:15:32.583 --> 00:15:34.759
Am I boring you. Mr. Spock?

256
00:15:34.803 --> 00:15:36.283
Decidedly not.

257
00:15:43.725 --> 00:15:45.161
Your move.

258
00:15:46.902 --> 00:15:48.948
There is a matter
I've been meaning to discuss.

259
00:15:48.991 --> 00:15:51.037
Should I get a drink first?

260
00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:52.560
You sound serious.

261
00:15:52.603 --> 00:15:54.475
Starfleet is built
on a command hierarchy.

262
00:15:54.518 --> 00:15:58.000
Fraternization can lead to...
complications.

263
00:15:58.044 --> 00:16:00.718
- As such. there are protocols...
- You want to tell Starfleet
about us.

264
00:16:00.742 --> 00:16:03.440
If there is anything to tell.

265
00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:11.535
Your move.

266
00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:15.539
Relationships...

267
00:16:17.846 --> 00:16:21.067
at least for humans. can be...

268
00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:22.677
quantum things.

269
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.984
Think about Schroödinger's cat...

270
00:16:25.027 --> 00:16:27.377
They exist or

271
00:16:27.421 --> 00:16:29.510
they don't.

272
00:16:29.553 --> 00:16:31.860
- depending on
who's observing them.
- That is...

273
00:16:31.903 --> 00:16:34.819
Not how quantum superposition
actually works?

274
00:16:34.863 --> 00:16:36.647
Yeah. I know.

275
00:16:36.691 --> 00:16:38.998
It's a-a metaphor. Spock.

276
00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:41.739
A messy one.
Just like

277
00:16:41.783 --> 00:16:43.654
whatever this is.

278
00:16:44.612 --> 00:16:46.048
My point is

279
00:16:46.092 --> 00:16:49.182
if we let Starfleet...

280
00:16:49.225 --> 00:16:51.184
Or anyone else.
for that matter...

281
00:16:51.227 --> 00:16:53.795
Look inside the box. then...

282
00:16:54.796 --> 00:16:57.059
Our quantum cat

283
00:16:57.103 --> 00:16:59.192
might disappear.

284
00:16:59.235 --> 00:17:01.803
I'd just as soon let it live.

285
00:17:01.846 --> 00:17:03.457
see how big it grows.

286
00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.155
- Your move.
- Excuse me.

287
00:17:07.374 --> 00:17:10.637
- Ensign. aren't you...
- Supposed to be resting. sir.
yes. but...

288
00:17:10.681 --> 00:17:14.076
Everybody ignores the "orders"
part of doctor's orders.

289
00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:15.904
I don't have
deuterium poisoning.

290
00:17:15.946 --> 00:17:18.166
When I saw Hemmer
on the turbolift.

291
00:17:18.211 --> 00:17:20.102
I heard the same sound
that I heard on the bridge.

292
00:17:20.126 --> 00:17:22.650
but I heard it on the bridge
before I went into the nacelle.

293
00:17:22.693 --> 00:17:24.694
before I was exposed
to deuterium.

294
00:17:24.739 --> 00:17:27.220
You believe your hallucinations
began on the bridge.

295
00:17:27.262 --> 00:17:29.352
Logic would suggest that

296
00:17:29.396 --> 00:17:31.267
the simplest solution is often

297
00:17:31.311 --> 00:17:33.400
the correct one. right?

298
00:17:33.443 --> 00:17:36.316
You seem to be the only
Enterprisecrewmember affected.

299
00:17:36.359 --> 00:17:37.752
Scans show nothing in the sector

300
00:17:37.795 --> 00:17:39.362
that would explain
your symptoms.

301
00:17:39.406 --> 00:17:41.712
Logic would suggest
exposure to a known hallucinogen

302
00:17:41.756 --> 00:17:43.932
is a more likely source
of hallucinations than...

303
00:17:43.975 --> 00:17:46.326
Than nothing at all.

304
00:17:46.369 --> 00:17:48.328
Yeah. I looked at your chart.
Nyota.

305
00:17:48.371 --> 00:17:50.330
It's... You have a textbook case

306
00:17:50.373 --> 00:17:52.462
of overwork and exhaustion.

307
00:17:52.506 --> 00:17:54.464
I can prescribe you something
to help you sleep.

308
00:17:54.508 --> 00:17:57.859
Sleeping is the last thing
I want to do right now.

309
00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:04.779
Saurian brandy.

310
00:18:04.822 --> 00:18:06.172
Coming right up.

311
00:18:09.175 --> 00:18:12.134
Your. uh. Vulcan buddy
should protect his queen.

312
00:18:13.918 --> 00:18:15.659
He was two moves away
from checkmate

313
00:18:15.703 --> 00:18:18.009
until you distracted him.
now he's in trouble.

314
00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.665
James Kirk.

315
00:18:21.709 --> 00:18:24.277
Uhura.
You're Sam's brother.

316
00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.714
Oh.
You're friends with Sam.

317
00:18:26.757 --> 00:18:28.237
Look.

318
00:18:28.281 --> 00:18:30.065
I really don't want
to be hit on right now.

319
00:18:30.109 --> 00:18:32.894
Oh.
You're friends with Sam.

320
00:18:33.895 --> 00:18:35.636
Come on.

321
00:18:35.679 --> 00:18:36.985
Nobody's hitting on anyone.

322
00:18:37.028 --> 00:18:38.595
You sat next to me.

323
00:18:38.639 --> 00:18:41.772
You just look like
you could use a friend.

324
00:18:42.817 --> 00:18:44.384
I appreciate the sentiment.

325
00:18:44.427 --> 00:18:47.909
but I am not in the market
for friends right now.

326
00:19:17.112 --> 00:19:19.984
It's not real.
It's not real.

327
00:19:20.855 --> 00:19:24.206
It's not real.

328
00:19:48.187 --> 00:19:49.623
I told you

329
00:19:49.666 --> 00:19:52.191
I am not hitting on you.

330
00:20:01.678 --> 00:20:03.593
I am so sorry.

331
00:20:03.637 --> 00:20:05.832
- I can't believe I...
- Yeah. I can't believe it.
either.

332
00:20:05.856 --> 00:20:07.510
You seem so pleasant.

333
00:20:08.555 --> 00:20:09.773
I will walk you to sickbay.

334
00:20:09.817 --> 00:20:11.253
Dr. M'Benga will fix you up.

335
00:20:11.297 --> 00:20:13.342
Whoa. hold on. You really
want to take me to sickbay

336
00:20:13.386 --> 00:20:15.126
and explain all this?

337
00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:16.843
I mean. it looked to me
like you were seeing something

338
00:20:16.867 --> 00:20:18.086
that wasn't there.

339
00:20:18.129 --> 00:20:19.914
You have big enough problems
to worry about

340
00:20:19.957 --> 00:20:22.197
without being written up
for striking a superior officer.

341
00:20:22.221 --> 00:20:25.572
I have a dermal regenerator
in my quarters.

342
00:20:25.615 --> 00:20:28.052
- Come on.
- Ow. ow.

343
00:20:28.096 --> 00:20:30.707
- You're making it worse.
- You're being a baby.

344
00:20:30.751 --> 00:20:33.710
- Ah...
- I'm done.

345
00:20:33.754 --> 00:20:37.061
I think your nightmare visions
are turning you into a sadist.

346
00:20:38.193 --> 00:20:40.587
You think it's all in my head.

347
00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.546
Of course not.

348
00:20:44.591 --> 00:20:47.724
Look. I-I've had
deuterium poisoning.

349
00:20:47.768 --> 00:20:49.552
and I've gone days
without sleep.

350
00:20:49.596 --> 00:20:51.598
but I have never
punched an officer

351
00:20:51.641 --> 00:20:52.599
because of either one.

352
00:20:52.642 --> 00:20:54.470
The point is

353
00:20:54.514 --> 00:20:56.429
I'm an exquisite
judge of character.

354
00:20:56.472 --> 00:20:57.821
and I believe you.

355
00:20:57.865 --> 00:20:59.693
There's something else
going on here.

356
00:20:59.736 --> 00:21:01.956
And if you'll let me.
I can help you figure out what.

357
00:21:01.999 --> 00:21:05.394
Okay. I've triple-checked
the sensor logs.

358
00:21:05.438 --> 00:21:07.396
and there's nothing
but deuterium out there.

359
00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:10.921
But something must be
doing this to me.

360
00:21:10.965 --> 00:21:13.141
No one else has reported
the same symptoms?

361
00:21:13.184 --> 00:21:14.664
M'Benga says I'm the only one.

362
00:21:14.708 --> 00:21:16.579
On the Enterprise. anyway.

363
00:21:16.623 --> 00:21:18.233
Good idea.

364
00:21:18.277 --> 00:21:20.148
I'll check in
with the Farragut'sdoctor.

365
00:21:20.191 --> 00:21:22.672
Can I bring her
your medical record?

366
00:21:28.112 --> 00:21:29.592
You gonna be okay alone?

367
00:21:30.854 --> 00:21:32.900
Yeah. Just. uh...

368
00:21:35.337 --> 00:21:38.297
don't punch any more officers
until I get back.

369
00:21:53.399 --> 00:21:55.401
Well. this would be a lot easier

370
00:21:55.444 --> 00:21:58.317
if you'd gotten the station's
lighting grid on line.

371
00:21:58.360 --> 00:21:59.796
Or the internal sensors.

372
00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.711
Yes. but if I had done that.

373
00:22:01.755 --> 00:22:04.714
we might have just exploded
and never known why.

374
00:22:08.979 --> 00:22:11.634
It's too close.

375
00:22:11.678 --> 00:22:13.636
It's too close. it's too close.
it can't breathe.

376
00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.899
Our saboteur is Starfleet?

377
00:22:15.943 --> 00:22:17.248
What is he saying?

378
00:22:19.512 --> 00:22:21.122
Get away from me.

379
00:22:21.165 --> 00:22:23.342
Get him away from me.
Get him away!

380
00:22:24.343 --> 00:22:25.518
There's nothing there.

381
00:22:25.561 --> 00:22:27.258
Nobody's gonna hurt you.

382
00:22:27.302 --> 00:22:29.478
This isn't real.

383
00:22:29.522 --> 00:22:33.047
It can't be real.
It can't be real.

384
00:22:41.708 --> 00:22:43.187
Are you real?

385
00:22:48.105 --> 00:22:50.369
What's your name?

386
00:22:50.412 --> 00:22:52.371
Ramon.

387
00:22:52.414 --> 00:22:54.373
Saul Ramon.

388
00:22:54.416 --> 00:22:57.811
Lieutenant Ramon. why
did you sabotage the station?

389
00:22:57.854 --> 00:23:00.596
Sabotage?

390
00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:02.946
That... that isn't real.

391
00:23:04.165 --> 00:23:05.906
It's-it's not real.

392
00:23:07.168 --> 00:23:09.910
Enterprise. we have
a medical emergency.

393
00:23:35.022 --> 00:23:36.763
What's going on. Captain?

394
00:23:36.806 --> 00:23:39.287
Contact Starfleet. We're
being attacked by an enemy...

395
00:23:52.039 --> 00:23:53.693
Ensign Uhura.

396
00:23:53.736 --> 00:23:54.824
Ensign Uhura.

397
00:23:58.567 --> 00:24:01.222
You're supposed to be
on medical leave. are you not?

398
00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:11.145
As your captain. I need
all my officers healthy.

399
00:24:11.188 --> 00:24:13.016
both physically and mentally.

400
00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:14.931
As your friend.

401
00:24:14.975 --> 00:24:17.064
I just want to help you
understand what's happening.

402
00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:22.722
You know.
the irony's not lost on me.

403
00:24:24.288 --> 00:24:25.899
The communications officer

404
00:24:25.942 --> 00:24:28.031
who can't communicate
what's wrong with her.

405
00:24:29.468 --> 00:24:31.818
Come in.

406
00:24:34.342 --> 00:24:35.667
Lieutenant Kirk.

407
00:24:35.691 --> 00:24:37.040
That's right.

408
00:24:37.084 --> 00:24:38.999
It's an honor to meet you. sir.

409
00:24:39.042 --> 00:24:41.436
Congratulations on
your promotion to Fleet Captain.

410
00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:43.090
Uh. it's temporary.

411
00:24:43.743 --> 00:24:45.222
I came to find Ensign Uhura.

412
00:24:45.266 --> 00:24:47.529
I ran into her earlier
and I was concerned.

413
00:24:47.573 --> 00:24:49.488
- As am I.
- As am I.

414
00:24:53.535 --> 00:24:55.015
Not the time. got it.

415
00:24:56.059 --> 00:24:59.454
I heard one of the station's
crew was arrested for sabotage.

416
00:24:59.498 --> 00:25:01.804
That's right. I had him beamed
to sickbay for evaluation.

417
00:25:01.848 --> 00:25:05.199
Farragut'smedical officer
got a call about him yesterday.

418
00:25:05.242 --> 00:25:07.244
One of his friends
was worried about him.

419
00:25:07.288 --> 00:25:08.898
said he kept talking about
seeing things

420
00:25:08.942 --> 00:25:09.943
that weren't there.

421
00:25:09.986 --> 00:25:12.119
Ring a bell?

422
00:25:19.822 --> 00:25:21.520
We need to know
what you saw. Lieutenant.

423
00:25:21.563 --> 00:25:23.434
It's the only way
we can help you.

424
00:25:23.478 --> 00:25:25.567
He might not understand us.

425
00:25:25.611 --> 00:25:27.221
There's significant damage

426
00:25:27.264 --> 00:25:30.006
to the speech and language
centers of his brain.

427
00:25:32.966 --> 00:25:35.185
And it's getting worse.

428
00:25:35.229 --> 00:25:37.231
His auditory nerves
are firing wildly.

429
00:25:50.723 --> 00:25:52.594
I'm fine.
Go after him.

430
00:25:52.638 --> 00:25:54.335
Security to Deck Four!

431
00:25:55.641 --> 00:25:57.643
This isn't real.

432
00:25:57.686 --> 00:25:59.253
Nyota. wake up. this isn't real.

433
00:25:59.296 --> 00:26:00.733
- No. hey. Uhura.
- This isn't...

434
00:26:00.776 --> 00:26:02.561
This is very real. okay?

435
00:26:02.604 --> 00:26:04.737
You aren't imagining it.

436
00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:06.608
Okay.

437
00:26:06.652 --> 00:26:10.090
We have to stop Ramon
before he hurts anyone else.

438
00:26:10.133 --> 00:26:12.005
Come on.

439
00:26:26.149 --> 00:26:28.412
Emergency lighting activated.

440
00:27:12.065 --> 00:27:14.589
Sickbay. we need
immediate medical assistance

441
00:27:14.633 --> 00:27:16.156
outside Astrometrics.

442
00:27:16.199 --> 00:27:18.419
Acknowledged. sending a medic.

443
00:27:18.462 --> 00:27:20.377
Captain.

444
00:27:20.421 --> 00:27:21.988
Ramon cut the power conduits.

445
00:27:22.031 --> 00:27:23.511
That's why it went dark.

446
00:27:23.554 --> 00:27:24.967
Definitely gonna make
finding him harder.

447
00:27:24.991 --> 00:27:26.296
Stay with her.

448
00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:28.385
Ramon's headed
towards Engineering.

449
00:27:37.743 --> 00:27:39.701
You okay?

450
00:27:39.745 --> 00:27:41.747
I'm fine.

451
00:27:46.012 --> 00:27:48.928
Okay. maybe not.

452
00:27:51.582 --> 00:27:53.149
James?

453
00:28:12.255 --> 00:28:13.779
Hey.

454
00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:16.259
I got you.
What happened?

455
00:28:16.303 --> 00:28:19.132
Sorry. I can't. I should
just go back to sickbay.

456
00:28:19.175 --> 00:28:21.830
-I'll walk back with you.
Come on.
-No.

457
00:28:21.874 --> 00:28:23.745
You keep searching for Ramon.

458
00:28:23.789 --> 00:28:25.529
I'll be fine.

459
00:28:25.573 --> 00:28:27.662
I promise.

460
00:28:55.908 --> 00:28:57.561
James?

461
00:28:57.605 --> 00:28:58.954
You two know each other?

462
00:28:58.998 --> 00:29:00.497
Now's not the time
to explain. Captain.

463
00:29:00.521 --> 00:29:02.392
If you haven't
seen Ramon. then...

464
00:29:02.436 --> 00:29:04.133
He must have found
a hole to hide in.

465
00:29:04.177 --> 00:29:05.744
We should double back.

466
00:29:38.864 --> 00:29:40.517
Uhura to all teams.

467
00:29:40.561 --> 00:29:41.954
I may have found
Lieutenant Ramon

468
00:29:41.997 --> 00:29:43.825
in the access tunnel
to the port nacelle.

469
00:29:43.869 --> 00:29:45.914
I'm not far. Wait for me.

470
00:30:04.019 --> 00:30:06.152
Step away from the console.

471
00:30:12.158 --> 00:30:14.900
Listen. the things
you've been seeing...

472
00:30:14.943 --> 00:30:17.685
I've been seeing them. too.
I understand.

473
00:30:17.728 --> 00:30:20.253
But whatever it is you saw
that made you come here.

474
00:30:20.296 --> 00:30:22.385
it wasn't real.

475
00:30:22.429 --> 00:30:24.910
None of it.

476
00:30:24.953 --> 00:30:27.390
But I am.

477
00:30:29.653 --> 00:30:31.742
My name is Nyota.

478
00:30:31.786 --> 00:30:33.179
I'm the communications officer.

479
00:30:33.222 --> 00:30:35.442
I-I was born in Kenya.

480
00:30:35.485 --> 00:30:37.357
I used to have a cat
named Kamili.

481
00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:38.924
My-my first memory

482
00:30:38.967 --> 00:30:41.883
is watching my dad
play the piano.

483
00:30:41.927 --> 00:30:44.494
I'm real.

484
00:30:48.803 --> 00:30:52.415
Ramon. please.

485
00:30:52.459 --> 00:30:54.853
I am begging you.

486
00:30:54.896 --> 00:30:57.638
Step away from the console.

487
00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:03.687
I don't want to blow
either of us up. Please!

488
00:31:03.731 --> 00:31:06.342
Fuel pod ejection
sequence initiated.

489
00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:17.092
-Emergency transport.
Beam us out. now.
-Wait!

490
00:31:36.677 --> 00:31:39.114
Two dead. chief medical officer
in surgery.

491
00:31:39.158 --> 00:31:42.117
the ship almost disabled.
and we have no idea why.

492
00:31:42.161 --> 00:31:44.250
When Ramon was in sickbay.
he seemed to be hearing

493
00:31:44.293 --> 00:31:46.339
the same sound
I've been hearing.

494
00:31:50.082 --> 00:31:51.692
The way I see it.
that's good news.

495
00:31:51.735 --> 00:31:53.259
You're not crazy.

496
00:31:54.695 --> 00:31:57.567
Something is affecting you.
the same way it affected him.

497
00:31:58.612 --> 00:32:00.309
And we're gonna figure out what.

498
00:32:02.703 --> 00:32:05.662
I think you should confine me
to my quarters. Captain.

499
00:32:05.706 --> 00:32:07.621
Why?

500
00:32:07.664 --> 00:32:09.579
How long until
I'm sabotaging the ship

501
00:32:09.623 --> 00:32:12.191
- or jumping out of an airlock?
- Oh.

502
00:32:13.235 --> 00:32:15.542
It won't come to that.
I promise you.

503
00:32:15.585 --> 00:32:17.283
Now I need to talk
to Commander Chin-Riley

504
00:32:17.326 --> 00:32:19.633
and find out how much damage
Ramon did to the refinery.

505
00:32:19.676 --> 00:32:21.156
Getting it on line is critical.

506
00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.985
But since
Lieutenant Ramon is deceased.

507
00:32:24.029 --> 00:32:26.857
Starfleet gave me permission
to decrypt his personal logs

508
00:32:26.901 --> 00:32:28.468
and private medical files.

509
00:32:29.643 --> 00:32:31.950
Maybe there's answers in there.

510
00:32:46.442 --> 00:32:49.010
James Kirk.
Sam's brother.

511
00:32:49.054 --> 00:32:51.447
La'An Noonien-Singh.

512
00:32:51.491 --> 00:32:54.885
Stickler for
orderly security records.

513
00:32:54.929 --> 00:32:56.713
Were-were you injured?

514
00:32:56.757 --> 00:32:59.890
Uh. no. I am just. uh.
waiting for Ensign Uhura.

515
00:32:59.934 --> 00:33:01.675
Oh. Right.

516
00:33:01.718 --> 00:33:04.199
- I didn't know you were friends.
- Oh. we just met.

517
00:33:04.243 --> 00:33:06.636
I happened to be there
when she needed some help.

518
00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:09.813
Although she seems like
the type who has trouble

519
00:33:09.857 --> 00:33:11.859
asking anyone for help.
especially her friends.

520
00:33:11.902 --> 00:33:14.557
And you're the type who can't
walk past a stranger in need.

521
00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:23.305
What?

522
00:33:27.353 --> 00:33:29.964
My. uh. my dad was in Starfleet.

523
00:33:30.008 --> 00:33:32.053
My mom. my brother and I.

524
00:33:32.097 --> 00:33:34.142
we spent my whole childhood
chasing him

525
00:33:34.186 --> 00:33:37.232
from one posting to another.

526
00:33:37.276 --> 00:33:39.017
barely seeing him.

527
00:33:40.366 --> 00:33:43.021
When I'd ask why.

528
00:33:43.064 --> 00:33:45.501
my mom would always say.

529
00:33:45.545 --> 00:33:48.461
"He's helping people
who really need it."

530
00:33:51.464 --> 00:33:53.683
But when you're a kid.
you think...

531
00:33:53.727 --> 00:33:56.164
Why does he care more about
strangers than he does about me?

532
00:34:01.561 --> 00:34:03.432
So you tell yourself.

533
00:34:03.476 --> 00:34:07.175
"Hey. if my dad chose
helping total strangers over me.

534
00:34:07.219 --> 00:34:10.351
it must... must be important."

535
00:34:10.396 --> 00:34:13.007
As one of those strangers
that Starfleet helped.

536
00:34:13.051 --> 00:34:15.661
I promise you...

537
00:34:15.705 --> 00:34:17.228
it is.

538
00:34:22.364 --> 00:34:24.322
I. uh. I should
finish my rounds.

539
00:34:25.715 --> 00:34:27.195
Good evening. Lieutenant.

540
00:34:30.068 --> 00:34:32.896
I haven't forgotten that drink
you owe me. by the way.

541
00:34:40.687 --> 00:34:44.212
Looks like the routing system
is almost back on line.

542
00:34:46.388 --> 00:34:50.478
You should be on Enterprise.
supervising the repairs there.

543
00:34:50.523 --> 00:34:53.221
That's what I told him.
but Captain Pike disagrees.

544
00:34:53.264 --> 00:34:55.963
Getting the station on line
remains the first priority.

545
00:34:57.399 --> 00:35:00.837
I got to know... what exactly
is your problem with me?

546
00:35:00.881 --> 00:35:03.101
I don't have a problem with you.

547
00:35:03.144 --> 00:35:05.320
I have a problem
with the situation.

548
00:35:05.364 --> 00:35:07.627
That is malarkey.

549
00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:09.368
and you know it.

550
00:35:10.412 --> 00:35:12.458
You're sloppy.
Okay?

551
00:35:12.501 --> 00:35:13.676
You don't respect protocols.

552
00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:15.200
You're too loose
with discipline.

553
00:35:15.243 --> 00:35:16.568
You don't follow orders
unless you feel like it.

554
00:35:16.592 --> 00:35:19.682
and you have crumbs
on your uniform.

555
00:35:19.726 --> 00:35:21.467
When did you even eat?

556
00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:23.338
You're like...

557
00:35:23.382 --> 00:35:24.513
a space hippie.

558
00:35:24.557 --> 00:35:25.993
Ooh.

559
00:35:26.036 --> 00:35:28.387
Well. I have been
called more names

560
00:35:28.430 --> 00:35:30.519
than there are stars in the sky.

561
00:35:30.563 --> 00:35:33.783
but "space hippie"
is a new one on me.

562
00:35:33.827 --> 00:35:35.698
I think you've gotten
too used to being

563
00:35:35.742 --> 00:35:37.831
the smartest person in the room.

564
00:35:37.874 --> 00:35:40.194
I don't think I'm
the smartest person in the room.

565
00:35:40.225 --> 00:35:41.835
Oh. sure you do.

566
00:35:41.878 --> 00:35:44.881
You hide behind
order and discipline

567
00:35:44.925 --> 00:35:48.755
because it kills you
when someone has the nerve

568
00:35:48.798 --> 00:35:51.671
to question your decisions.

569
00:35:51.714 --> 00:35:54.891
You don't think
my years of experience

570
00:35:54.935 --> 00:35:57.067
should buy me any deference?

571
00:35:57.111 --> 00:35:59.461
You've been in Starfleet
since before I was born.

572
00:35:59.505 --> 00:36:01.071
but I outrank you.

573
00:36:01.115 --> 00:36:03.770
Why do you think that is?

574
00:36:09.341 --> 00:36:13.171
I'll have the station on line
in a few hours.

575
00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:17.566
I swear I saw John
standing in the mess hall.

576
00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.177
like he never...

577
00:36:20.526 --> 00:36:22.180
Like he was still alive.

578
00:36:22.223 --> 00:36:23.659
And I tried
to say something to him.

579
00:36:23.703 --> 00:36:24.941
and everyone just stared at me.

580
00:36:24.965 --> 00:36:27.576
They thought I'd lost my mind.

581
00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:29.317
And that noise...

582
00:36:29.361 --> 00:36:32.320
I think I am losing my mind.

583
00:36:36.977 --> 00:36:39.719
He was having the same kind
of hallucinations as me.

584
00:36:40.894 --> 00:36:43.592
Hearing the same sound.
His...

585
00:36:43.636 --> 00:36:45.855
his brain scans show activity

586
00:36:45.899 --> 00:36:47.640
in the same region.

587
00:36:47.683 --> 00:36:49.076
But his case was more severe.

588
00:36:49.119 --> 00:36:51.426
Or it just started earlier.

589
00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:53.776
I mean. based on the date
of these logs.

590
00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:55.256
I may be...

591
00:36:58.477 --> 00:37:02.132
a day and a half
from losing my mind entirely.

592
00:37:02.176 --> 00:37:04.396
Look. why don't we just
take a break. all right?

593
00:37:04.439 --> 00:37:06.876
Update Captain Pike
on our progress.

594
00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:09.531
or lack thereof.

595
00:37:09.575 --> 00:37:11.577
Maybe get something to eat.

596
00:37:11.620 --> 00:37:13.753
I hear the mess
is serving real cookies.

597
00:37:13.796 --> 00:37:15.668
not from the matter synthesizer.

598
00:37:15.711 --> 00:37:17.713
I don't need a cookie.

599
00:37:17.757 --> 00:37:20.020
Okay. now you sound crazy.

600
00:37:23.632 --> 00:37:28.158
I'm sorry. it was a dumb joke.

601
00:37:30.335 --> 00:37:31.727
It's not a joke.

602
00:37:32.902 --> 00:37:34.730
That's the problem.

603
00:37:38.473 --> 00:37:42.303
This isn't the first time
I've seen something like this.

604
00:37:43.783 --> 00:37:47.569
Imagined someone
who isn't there.

605
00:37:50.311 --> 00:37:51.834
A few years ago.

606
00:37:51.878 --> 00:37:54.402
I lost my parents and my brother

607
00:37:54.446 --> 00:37:56.622
in a shuttle accident.

608
00:37:56.665 --> 00:37:59.146
I-I wasn't there
when it happened.

609
00:37:59.189 --> 00:38:01.148
I never saw
the crash site. but...

610
00:38:02.149 --> 00:38:04.282
after they died.

611
00:38:04.325 --> 00:38:07.241
it felt like every time

612
00:38:07.285 --> 00:38:08.938
I closed my eyes.

613
00:38:08.982 --> 00:38:10.810
I'd see it.

614
00:38:12.333 --> 00:38:15.858
The crash. their final moments.

615
00:38:16.903 --> 00:38:20.167
I don't know
what to say. except...

616
00:38:20.210 --> 00:38:22.648
I'm sorry.

617
00:38:22.691 --> 00:38:27.348
Got so bad I couldn't even
look at pictures of them.

618
00:38:27.392 --> 00:38:31.352
Just seeing their faces...

619
00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:37.576
And then Hemmer died.
and it all came back.

620
00:38:38.577 --> 00:38:43.625
But I kept covering it with work
and smiles and more work.

621
00:38:43.669 --> 00:38:46.672
The truth is I've never
been able to face death.

622
00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:51.677
Everyone has some way
of dealing with it

623
00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:55.855
and moving on. but I just...

624
00:38:57.596 --> 00:39:00.903
I don't know how.

625
00:39:00.947 --> 00:39:02.949
How can I be a Starfleet officer

626
00:39:02.992 --> 00:39:05.343
if I can't handle death?

627
00:39:06.387 --> 00:39:08.607
Maybe you can't.

628
00:39:11.044 --> 00:39:12.654
Look. I could tell you

629
00:39:12.698 --> 00:39:15.570
some comforting fairy tale.

630
00:39:15.614 --> 00:39:18.399
but we both know the truth.

631
00:39:18.443 --> 00:39:22.142
Our job puts us up against death

632
00:39:22.185 --> 00:39:24.579
more than is fair.
And we might not like it.

633
00:39:24.623 --> 00:39:26.668
but we do have to face it.

634
00:39:28.061 --> 00:39:30.716
And right now. death is winning.

635
00:39:31.891 --> 00:39:33.327
It claimed your family.

636
00:39:33.371 --> 00:39:35.764
it claimed your friend.

637
00:39:35.808 --> 00:39:38.332
It convinced you to forget them.

638
00:39:38.376 --> 00:39:41.466
because it's less painful than
holding on to their memories.

639
00:39:43.772 --> 00:39:46.558
Now. you can let death win...

640
00:39:47.776 --> 00:39:49.648
or you can fight back.

641
00:39:51.127 --> 00:39:53.129
Hold on to them.

642
00:39:57.438 --> 00:39:58.874
Still no to that cookie?

643
00:40:05.228 --> 00:40:07.230
Be right back.

644
00:40:23.812 --> 00:40:25.161
Not that!

645
00:40:29.209 --> 00:40:31.646
Important lesson:
be less gullible.

646
00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:34.083
Hey!

647
00:40:34.127 --> 00:40:36.608
Let's start with
dialing down the gain

648
00:40:36.651 --> 00:40:38.784
on the subspace antenna.

649
00:40:38.827 --> 00:40:41.700
or else you'll
burn out the receiver.

650
00:40:46.705 --> 00:40:48.533
Burn out the receiver.

651
00:40:56.715 --> 00:40:58.760
Got the last one.
Thought we could split it.

652
00:40:58.804 --> 00:41:01.720
Forget about the stupid cookie
for a second.

653
00:41:01.763 --> 00:41:04.070
I think I figured something out.

654
00:41:04.113 --> 00:41:05.811
Ramon's brain scan.

655
00:41:05.854 --> 00:41:07.813
What am I supposed
to be noticing?

656
00:41:07.856 --> 00:41:10.990
There was damage to the language
center of his brain.

657
00:41:11.033 --> 00:41:14.384
My brain scan shows elevated
activity in the same spot.

658
00:41:14.428 --> 00:41:17.692
And we were both hearing
that same sound.

659
00:41:17.736 --> 00:41:20.608
What if someone or something

660
00:41:20.652 --> 00:41:23.263
was trying to communicate
with him.

661
00:41:23.306 --> 00:41:24.656
with me.

662
00:41:24.699 --> 00:41:26.440
but their signal's too strong?

663
00:41:27.659 --> 00:41:31.706
So strong it that burned out
that area of his brain entirely.

664
00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:33.447
It's a message from some...

665
00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:36.406
invisible alien?

666
00:41:39.584 --> 00:41:41.542
You want to talk to Sam.
don't you?

667
00:41:42.587 --> 00:41:43.955
Your symptoms started
once we arrived

668
00:41:43.979 --> 00:41:46.286
in the nebula. right?

669
00:41:46.329 --> 00:41:48.941
So it makes sense that our
mystery alien would live here.

670
00:41:48.984 --> 00:41:51.291
But our sensors aren't
showing any life-forms.

671
00:41:51.334 --> 00:41:52.945
Well. there's a theory...

672
00:41:52.988 --> 00:41:55.251
It's a little bit fringe.
but hear me out...

673
00:41:55.295 --> 00:41:57.297
That extra-dimensional
life-forms

674
00:41:57.340 --> 00:41:59.778
could actually
poke into our space

675
00:41:59.821 --> 00:42:02.824
and attach themselves
to atoms from our dimension.

676
00:42:02.868 --> 00:42:04.783
Meaning they aren't
sophisticated life-forms

677
00:42:04.826 --> 00:42:07.568
capable of speech
or even language.

678
00:42:07.612 --> 00:42:09.570
There are similarities

679
00:42:09.614 --> 00:42:13.095
in the ways different species
process thoughts. ideas.

680
00:42:13.139 --> 00:42:14.769
That's how the universal
translator works...

681
00:42:14.793 --> 00:42:16.969
By recognizing
those similarities.

682
00:42:17.012 --> 00:42:20.538
Maybe my brain
is doing the same thing?

683
00:42:20.581 --> 00:42:22.714
Invisible aliens
are using your brain

684
00:42:22.757 --> 00:42:24.411
as a universal translator.

685
00:42:24.454 --> 00:42:26.848
It might be the only way
they know how to communicate.

686
00:42:26.892 --> 00:42:29.895
They send simple ideas to
the language center of my brain.

687
00:42:29.938 --> 00:42:32.245
then my brain interprets

688
00:42:32.288 --> 00:42:34.160
those ideas the only way
it can...

689
00:42:34.203 --> 00:42:36.771
Through my thoughts.
my memories.

690
00:42:36.815 --> 00:42:38.381
The visions.

691
00:42:38.425 --> 00:42:41.210
they're like vocabulary.

692
00:42:41.254 --> 00:42:42.908
What are they trying to say?

693
00:42:46.868 --> 00:42:49.392
I felt the walls closing in
because they feel

694
00:42:49.436 --> 00:42:50.872
trapped.

695
00:42:52.134 --> 00:42:54.833
I was attacking myself because

696
00:42:54.876 --> 00:42:56.922
we're responsible.

697
00:42:57.923 --> 00:42:59.446
They showed me
the ship exploding

698
00:42:59.489 --> 00:43:01.013
because they are desperate

699
00:43:01.056 --> 00:43:03.189
to break free. but...

700
00:43:04.364 --> 00:43:06.235
We're killing them.

701
00:43:06.279 --> 00:43:08.063
We're killing
the ones they love.

702
00:43:08.107 --> 00:43:09.543
and they're terrified.

703
00:43:09.587 --> 00:43:11.632
And that's what
they're making me feel.

704
00:43:13.286 --> 00:43:15.636
If they're living in the nebula.
in the deuterium...

705
00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:18.073
In a way.
they are the deuterium.

706
00:43:18.117 --> 00:43:20.075
So. then when
we pull the deuterium

707
00:43:20.119 --> 00:43:21.860
out of the nebula
into our nacelles.

708
00:43:21.903 --> 00:43:24.210
and when the station
processes and refines it...

709
00:43:24.253 --> 00:43:25.994
We're basically torturing them.

710
00:43:26.038 --> 00:43:27.866
Uhura to Captain Pike.

711
00:43:27.909 --> 00:43:30.651
Sir. we need to stop the fuel
refinery from being activated.

712
00:43:30.695 --> 00:43:32.934
I'm not sure what's going on.
Ensign. but it's too late.

713
00:43:32.958 --> 00:43:36.396
The refinery was activated
five minutes ago.

714
00:43:44.883 --> 00:43:46.841
Captain. you have to
shut down the station!

715
00:43:46.885 --> 00:43:48.669
It's a matter of life or death!

716
00:43:48.713 --> 00:43:51.672
I'll contact Una
and do what I can.

717
00:43:51.716 --> 00:43:53.195
Nothing's working.

718
00:43:53.239 --> 00:43:54.782
The refinery's automated systems
aren't responding

719
00:43:54.806 --> 00:43:57.025
to any commands.
It's not shutting down.

720
00:43:57.069 --> 00:43:59.593
Perhaps Lieutenant Ramon

721
00:43:59.637 --> 00:44:01.551
did more damage than we thought.

722
00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:07.514
Bridge.

723
00:44:46.553 --> 00:44:47.946
Keep going.

724
00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:10.882
You all right?

725
00:45:10.925 --> 00:45:13.754
The station's emergency shutdown
procedures aren't working.

726
00:45:13.798 --> 00:45:14.775
You mind telling me
why we need to

727
00:45:14.799 --> 00:45:16.452
shut it down in the first place?

728
00:45:16.496 --> 00:45:17.952
We believe there's
some sort of alien life-form...

729
00:45:17.976 --> 00:45:20.543
We're killing them. Captain.

730
00:45:25.984 --> 00:45:28.377
The creatures that live
in the deuterium in the nebula...

731
00:45:28.421 --> 00:45:30.075
We're killing them

732
00:45:30.118 --> 00:45:33.121
by pulling them into
our fuel pods and our nacelles.

733
00:45:33.165 --> 00:45:34.949
and now the station's
doing the same thing.

734
00:45:34.993 --> 00:45:37.082
but at a much larger scale.

735
00:45:37.125 --> 00:45:38.886
But we can't see them
or detect them in any way.

736
00:45:38.910 --> 00:45:41.826
I can hear them.

737
00:45:41.869 --> 00:45:43.218
So could Ramon.

738
00:45:43.262 --> 00:45:45.525
They're screaming for our help.

739
00:45:45.568 --> 00:45:49.181
Ramon died trying to save them.

740
00:45:49.224 --> 00:45:51.792
Captain. you told me
the point of this station

741
00:45:51.836 --> 00:45:54.795
was to help us find
new forms of life.

742
00:45:54.839 --> 00:45:56.884
But what is the point
of exploring

743
00:45:56.928 --> 00:45:58.973
if we're just gonna
kill what we find?

744
00:45:59.017 --> 00:46:00.148
We can't shut it down.

745
00:46:00.192 --> 00:46:02.107
Then we need to destroy it.

746
00:46:11.769 --> 00:46:13.205
How certain are you?

747
00:46:14.206 --> 00:46:17.513
I'm certain. Captain.

748
00:46:20.081 --> 00:46:21.866
What's the play?

749
00:46:22.867 --> 00:46:24.433
Hail the refinery.

750
00:46:24.477 --> 00:46:26.784
order an emergency evacuation.

751
00:46:26.827 --> 00:46:29.961
then vent all the deuterium
from Enterprise'sfuel pods.

752
00:46:30.004 --> 00:46:32.180
Order the Farragut
to do the same.

753
00:46:33.181 --> 00:46:34.226
You heard her.

754
00:46:34.269 --> 00:46:36.097
Aye. sir.

755
00:46:36.141 --> 00:46:38.708
Attention. All refinery crew
and maintenance teams.

756
00:46:38.752 --> 00:46:41.711
evacuate immediately.
Repeat: all...

757
00:46:49.894 --> 00:46:51.373
No life signs aboard.

758
00:46:51.417 --> 00:46:53.158
All crew have safely
cleared the refinery.

759
00:46:53.201 --> 00:46:55.377
Fire torpedoes!

760
00:46:55.421 --> 00:46:57.205
Torpedoes away.

761
00:47:14.962 --> 00:47:16.659
Did that work?

762
00:47:20.359 --> 00:47:22.230
Uhura. what is it?

763
00:47:28.106 --> 00:47:30.151
We did it.

764
00:47:31.283 --> 00:47:33.328
They're safe.

765
00:47:36.636 --> 00:47:38.203
That's it?

766
00:47:39.421 --> 00:47:40.640
Yeah.

767
00:47:42.294 --> 00:47:44.122
That's it.

768
00:47:50.868 --> 00:47:54.828
The jumping-off point
for a new era of exploration.

769
00:47:55.829 --> 00:47:57.526
Well...

770
00:47:57.570 --> 00:47:59.877
Starfleet can build a
new gas station someplace else.

771
00:48:01.008 --> 00:48:04.011
One where there isn't anything
living in the deuterium.

772
00:48:04.055 --> 00:48:06.057
And if there's any blowback.
it's on me.

773
00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:08.276
End of the day.
that was my call. Okay?

774
00:48:09.843 --> 00:48:11.932
Or we can tell 'em
someone's brash influence

775
00:48:11.976 --> 00:48:13.716
rubbed off on you.

776
00:48:13.760 --> 00:48:16.241
Always happy to be of service.

777
00:48:16.284 --> 00:48:18.112
You know. I was thinking.

778
00:48:18.156 --> 00:48:20.941
you discovered
a completely new form of life.

779
00:48:21.942 --> 00:48:23.944
They might give you a medal.

780
00:48:23.988 --> 00:48:26.512
And now. if you don't mind.

781
00:48:26.555 --> 00:48:28.601
would you take your station.

782
00:48:31.430 --> 00:48:33.301
Hail the Farragut.

783
00:48:33.345 --> 00:48:35.105
As soon as we've collected
all of our escape pods.

784
00:48:35.129 --> 00:48:37.740
let's get out of this nebula
before we cause any more damage.

785
00:48:37.784 --> 00:48:40.221
And then I am going
to need Admiral Nagawa

786
00:48:40.265 --> 00:48:42.702
on my private channel.
And after that.

787
00:48:42.745 --> 00:48:44.182
go take a nap.

788
00:48:44.225 --> 00:48:46.010
That's an order.

789
00:49:03.636 --> 00:49:06.334
Starship Maintenance 307.

790
00:49:07.379 --> 00:49:09.859
Starship Maintenance.
what about it?

791
00:49:10.904 --> 00:49:13.037
You were my professor
at the Academy.

792
00:49:13.080 --> 00:49:15.169
Oh. I remember.

793
00:49:15.213 --> 00:49:16.562
You gave me a C.

794
00:49:18.651 --> 00:49:20.392
And you deserved that C.

795
00:49:20.435 --> 00:49:23.264
My final paper was
meticulously researched.

796
00:49:23.308 --> 00:49:25.832
It was sloppy.

797
00:49:25.875 --> 00:49:28.226
But for what it's worth.
that well-deserved C

798
00:49:28.269 --> 00:49:31.925
is absolutely not
what's been bothering you.

799
00:49:31.969 --> 00:49:33.448
It's Hemmer.

800
00:49:34.493 --> 00:49:38.018
I'm a reminder that your friend
died and I replaced him.

801
00:49:38.062 --> 00:49:41.021
And every time you see me.

802
00:49:41.065 --> 00:49:44.503
it dredges up
all of that sadness.

803
00:49:44.546 --> 00:49:46.766
I get it.

804
00:49:46.809 --> 00:49:49.943
I've been there.

805
00:49:49.987 --> 00:49:53.555
Too many times.

806
00:49:57.081 --> 00:50:00.910
And if you want to keep saying
it's the C.

807
00:50:00.954 --> 00:50:03.174
I don't have to contradict you.

808
00:50:33.334 --> 00:50:35.032
It's your family?

809
00:50:35.075 --> 00:50:37.730
My mother always knew
how to make me laugh.

810
00:50:40.167 --> 00:50:41.777
It's been a long time

811
00:50:41.821 --> 00:50:43.779
since I've been able
to look at that.

812
00:50:45.781 --> 00:50:48.088
To staying in the fight.

813
00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:56.401
Why do you think they chose me?

814
00:50:56.444 --> 00:50:59.012
You were exactly
what they needed.

815
00:50:59.056 --> 00:51:00.883
Thoughtful. empathetic.

816
00:51:00.927 --> 00:51:02.363
You heard them.

817
00:51:02.407 --> 00:51:05.540
I mean. maybe all it took
was a good listener.

818
00:51:07.673 --> 00:51:09.109
Well. I'm glad to see

819
00:51:09.153 --> 00:51:10.719
that everything
worked out for the best.

820
00:51:10.763 --> 00:51:12.939
And I think
I'm gonna write a paper

821
00:51:12.982 --> 00:51:15.202
about our deuterium friends.

822
00:51:15.246 --> 00:51:17.813
- Hey.
- Oh. Wow. Sounds thrilling.

823
00:51:20.686 --> 00:51:23.123
I've been wanting to say...

824
00:51:23.167 --> 00:51:25.299
the Farragut
is lucky to have you. Jim.

825
00:51:26.431 --> 00:51:29.042
I'm proud of you.
Honestly.

826
00:51:33.351 --> 00:51:34.874
Thanks. Sam.

827
00:51:43.274 --> 00:51:45.145
What is happening right now?

828
00:51:47.234 --> 00:51:49.018
I'm not sure. either.

829
00:51:49.062 --> 00:51:50.474
You're seriously
not gonna apologize?

830
00:51:50.498 --> 00:51:53.153
For what... for being
extremely competent?

831
00:51:53.197 --> 00:51:55.460
For being a...
Okay.

832
00:51:55.503 --> 00:51:57.679
You know what?
Forget it.

833
00:51:57.723 --> 00:51:59.812
Go have fun
on your stupid little ship.

834
00:51:59.855 --> 00:52:02.423
I'm gonna be here.
on the flagship.

835
00:52:06.514 --> 00:52:09.038
Well.

836
00:52:09.082 --> 00:52:11.345
- that was something.
- Yeah. it's just

837
00:52:11.389 --> 00:52:13.042
Sam being Sam.

838
00:52:13.086 --> 00:52:15.610
- Sometimes he can be. uh...
- Frustrating.

839
00:52:19.397 --> 00:52:21.138
James. meet our

840
00:52:21.181 --> 00:52:22.617
chief science officer.
Mr. Spock.

841
00:52:22.661 --> 00:52:24.053
Spock. meet James Kirk.

842
00:52:24.097 --> 00:52:25.664
first officer of the Farragut.

843
00:52:28.362 --> 00:52:30.799
Why don't you join us?

844
00:52:32.323 --> 00:52:34.673
Yeah.

